# steamos-update: a stub. It updates nothing.
#
# Steam's Gaming Mode runs this absolute path
# (/usr/bin/steamos-polkit-helpers/steamos-update) to ask about OS updates.
# On SteamOS that path is a real updater. Here it is missing, Steam exits 127,
# and first-run setup stops on a network error that is not a network error.
#
# Exit 7 is "already up to date" / "nothing to apply". Exit 0 on apply is what
# Pizzarchy measured as "an update was applied", which makes Steam reboot.
# This stub never exits 0 except for help.
#
# To update this system, from the desktop: omarchy update

set -euo pipefail

note() {
  printf 'steamos-update: %s\n' "$1" >&2
  logger -t steamos-update -- "$1" || true
}

case ${1-} in
  check)
    exit 7
    ;;
  --supports-duplicate-detection)
    exit 1
    ;;
  "" | apply)
    note "nothing to apply; this OS is updated with omarchy update"
    exit 7
    ;;
  -h | --help | help)
    cat <<'USAGE'
steamos-update (stub) — reports up to date and updates nothing.

Steam calls this path during Gaming Mode setup. It is not the SteamOS
updater, and it does not download, stage or apply an update.

  check                            exit 7 (already up to date)
  --supports-duplicate-detection   exit 1 (not supported)
  (no argument) | apply            exit 7 (nothing to apply)

Exit 0 on apply is not used. Steam reads 0 as "applied" and reboots.

To update this system, from the desktop: omarchy update
USAGE
    exit 0
    ;;
  *)
    note "unrecognised argument '$1'; answering up to date"
    exit 7
    ;;
esac
